Version: 0.0.8.9000
Date: 2026-09-23
R: 4.4.3
Studies: bench, boundary_se, coverage, power, mclogit, messy, imputation, brms
Date.coverage: 2026-09-23 and 2026-09-24
Date.power: 2026-09-23 and 2026-09-24
Date.mclogit: 2026-09-24
Date.messy: 2026-09-24
Date.imputation: 2026-09-24
Date.brms: 2026-09-24
Note: A COMPLETE retention set, at the parameters 0.0.7.9000 used: coverage
 and power 2000 replicates, mclogit 500, messy 400, boundary_se 400 per
 regime, bench 160, imputation 40, brms 3. Run against the 0.0.8.9000 builds
 of illume and illumex, installed in a scratch library; illumex's date
 features merged after them and are not in it. bench, boundary_se and most of
 coverage ran on 2026-09-23, the rest on 2026-09-24.
Note: The rule for a usable fit changed during this run, and every cell it
 could change was re-run. The coverage, power, mclogit and messy scripts
 counted an illume fit only when TMB called its Hessian positive definite
 (pdHess). A fit whose covariance sits at its boundary has the direction the
 data cannot resolve held and pdHess FALSE by design, and the package's own
 rule, ilm_fixed_usable(), counts it. Holding only the flat direction
 (adopted at 0.0.7.9000) holds every boundary fit, so the first run, with the
 old rule, kept 176 of 2000 mn_J5_thin replicates (conv_rate 0.088), 421 of
 2000 in mn_J3_ar and 57 of 400 fits in messy's flat_re. The scripts now ask
 what the package asks. The cells that had lost replicates were re-run on
 2026-09-24 -- nine of coverage, four of power and all of messy. Every other
 cell had kept all of its replicates, which the new rule cannot change, and
 mclogit lost none. The same rule understated 0.0.7.9000 too: its conv_rate
 column is the share with pdHess, not the share usable (see its messy note).
Note: Counted as the package counts them, the multinomial coverage cells use
 95.4% to 100% of their replicates and cover at 0.946 to 0.954. Most of those
 fits are held at a boundary -- 1731 of 1907 in mn_J5_thin, 1579 of 2000 in
 mn_J3_ar, 1365 of 1979 in mn_J5_mid, 669 of 1998 in mn_J5_rich -- and the
 held fits alone cover at 0.942 to 0.955 in those four cells: the
 flat-direction hold measured on its own fits. The re-run cells' CSVs record
 n_held and coverage_held.
Note: In power, mn_J3_thin uses 2000 replicates at every effect size where
 the first run used 1851 to 1893; its size is 0.045 (Wald) and 0.055 (LRT),
 and its power is within 0.003 of 0.0.7.9000's. Type I error ranges over
 0.042 to 0.061 (Wald) across the 13 cells, as at 0.0.7.9000. In messy,
 flat_re (a true SD of 0.05) uses 395 of 400 fits, where 0.0.7.9000 used 253
 -- its note predicted 97.5% -- and covers at 0.947, level with mclogit;
 rare uses 395 and combined 397, covering at 0.930 and 0.942 against
 mclogit's 0.853 and 0.894.
Note: The imputation study's lowrank_pa route called illume:::ilm_anom_rank(),
 which moved to illumex in the split. Inside a replicate the error became NA,
 so the first run fitted lowrank_pa in 0 of 40 replicates in three cells and
 13 of 40 in a fourth. The script now takes the function from illumex and
 resolves it before any replicate runs, so a future move stops the study.
 Re-run on 2026-09-24, every method, lowrank_pa included, reproduces
 0.0.7.9000 exactly.
Note: bench, boundary_se, mclogit and brms reproduce 0.0.7.9000's estimates
 exactly. In boundary_se the fit's own rule (B) now equals C3 in every
 boundary fit, as adopting C intended; at 0.0.7.9000 it ran 0.547 to 1.340
 times C3's. Bench timings are longer for every package -- gaussian_mm by
 1.20 to 1.21 times in illume, lme4 and glmmTMB alike -- which is load on the
 machine rather than a change in illume. Multinomial fits are the exception:
 illume's went from 9.1 to 10.7 times nnet's time at J = 3 and from 5.8 to
 7.2 at J = 5, about a fifth slower relative to nnet. Not yet explained.
Note: From 04:15 on 2026-09-24 the re-runs used 5 workers rather than 10.
 Every replicate sets its own seed, so only the secs columns are affected.
